u.s lawmakers have reiterated their  country's rock solid commitment to  taiwan in a surprise visit to the island  five lawmakers from both the republican  and democratic parties met taiwanese  president sanguen earlier on friday  their visit comes amid the worst  tensions in decades between china and  taiwan china has repeatedly sent  military aircraft and ships into  taiwanese territory through the past  year in what analysts view as  intimidation tactics  the visit by the u.s delegation is the  third by lawmakers from the united  states this year this is what their  leader said  our commitment to taiwan is rock solid  and has remained steadfast as the ties  between us has deepened  taiwan is a democratic success story  a reliable partner and a force for good  in the world  the visit will come as good news for  taiwan in this year of rising tensions  with china the united states does not  recognize taiwan as a country but is  still its most important arms supplier  taiwanese leader cyan spoke of the ties  between the island and the united states  in terms of the regional situation  taiwan will continue to step up  cooperation with the us uphold our  shared values of freedom and democracy  and ensure regional peace and stability  and for more on this i'm joined now by  my colleague william yang from dw's east  asia bureau in taipei william how  important are such visits for taiwan  yeah so today's visit and also the last  visit earlier this month both focus on  the dialogue between us lawmakers and  the defense minister  defense officials here in taiwan so i  think this is actually a very important  reassurance for the taiwanese government  and also the officials over here that  washington is very interesting a more  concrete and consistent and also a more  in-person dialogue  to talk about security measures not you  know  there are no details that were being  revealed obviously but i think the fact  that these consistent exchanges have  been focusing more on the imminent  threats coming from china or in the  region that will allow taiwan to  actually have a more concrete idea about  where the us government stands and how  much support they can count on coming  from washington let's talk a bit about  the chinese threat now the u.s is  legally committed to providing taiwan  with the military means to defend itself  from a potential chinese attack but the  us has never openly committed to coming  to taiwan's aid militarily in the event  of an attack is that the assurance that  taiwan wants but the u.s is not willing  to give  i think that taiwanese government right  now at least is very uh practical they  know that the us has  a lot of complicated situa political  factors that will prevent it to openly  be militarily committed to defending  taiwan so what they are also telling the  domestic audience here in taiwan is that  the biggest uh the ultimate  responsibility of defending taiwan will  risk with the taiwanese people but the  u.s is committed to continuing to supply  more modernized weapon systems and also  helping to train and also having  security  sessions that the taiwanese  soldiers can actually learn from their  u.s counterparts about how to operate  and how to use all these modernized and  advanced weapons in a  more battle-ready situation  uh now william we've spoken about this  uh earlier now this year so far china  has i think sent in more than a 150  warplanes into taiwanese airspace and  taiwan's defense minister also warned in  october that china could essentially  mount a full-scale invasion by 2025. now  given this warning and these rising  tensions how are taiwanese people  reacting  on a day-to-day basis i would say  taiwanese people are still not too  worried about the fa the immediate  danger that china might launch a  military attack i think the fact is that  they understand there are more  complicated factors that will  have to push china to that limit and  they know that the bottom line for china  is to actually try to reunify with  taiwan without having actually to resort  to the military option and also uh i  think the there is an interesting  increase among the younger generation  about the  uh willingness and also the  determination for them to actually join  the military trainings and also to be  ready to uh  help and uh  jump into a battle in case that such a  situation developed to that level and  the government is actually thinking and  planning to increase the frequency of  military trainings for uh usual citizens  so i think uh this is going to  allow taiwan's  society to be mentally and also i think  realistically more prepared but at the  same time they don't want to panic  themselves into a situation where they  live in fear every day  where does china come in in all of this  william how does china view these  actions and does it impact taiwan in the  short term  so china definitely do not want to see a  more stable and frequent and consistent  exchanges especially between security  officials between the u.s and taiwan and  they have again today  use a warning to say that the u.s should  not try to play the so-called taiwan car  to trigger and provoke china uh but i  think for beijing this is not the  situation where they try to use all  different kinds of ways to isolate  taiwan and to increase the intimidation  against taiwan is actually backfiring  for them and they are probably starting  to realize that but they have no way to  back off and try to soften their stance  obviously under xi jinping so in a lot  of the cases especially in lithuania for  example we're seeing china's continuing  that very hard-line way of retaliating  against countries that try to build up  more closer ties with taiwan but when it  comes to the us i think china finds  itself very  uh like  out of sort almost you know like they  don't really know how to discourage the  us government from uh like downgrading  or like stopping all these uh increased  exchanges so i think uh they will have  to figure out their own way of trying to  deal with this uh more normalized  interaction between the us and taiwan  william yang from dw's east asia bureau  in taipei always a pleasure talking to  you thank you so much for joining us  today  thank you
 

